From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: a733: name bank PH's I/O supply

Bank PH's pads are supplied from VCC-PH = SWOUT2 at 3.3 V
(from Allwinner's A733_PINOUT_V0.91 sheet "Pin
Description", column "I/O Power Supply[7]"; SWOUT2 is the rail the eMMC node
already consumes as its vmmc). Patch `arm64-dts-a733-bank-pc-io-supply` left this bank unwired on the
grounds that PH "is already at 3.3 V and reads back correctly; wiring it
changes nothing observable". That is measurably wrong, and why it is wrong
is the point of this patch.

Without the phandle the bank reaches the rail anyway
----------------------------------------------------
`sunxi_pmx_request()` builds the supply id as "vcc-p%c" from the bank index
(pinctrl-sunxi.c:1043) and calls regulator_get() (:1044). With no
vcc-ph-supply in the tree, regulator_dev_lookup() (regulator/core.c:2001)
falls past the phandle and the empty board map to its last resort,
regulator_lookup_by_name() (:2037) -> regulator_match() (:1961) ->
rdev_get_name() (:103), which compares against the DT property
regulator-name. SWOUT2 carries regulator-name = "vcc-ph", byte-equal to the
id the driver asks for, so the bank binds the real rail whenever that rail
is already registered -- and a successful match logs nothing at all. Only
the failing path prints "supply ... not found, using dummy regulator"
(core.c:2235).

The bank's per-request cache is cleared on release (pinctrl-sunxi.c:1091),
so a consumer whose probe defers after its pins were bound gives the bank
back -- dd.c:735 -> device_unbind_cleanup() -> devres_release_all() ->
devm_pinctrl_release() -> pinctrl_free() -> pinmux_disable_setting() ->
pin_free() -> sunxi_pmx_free() -- and the next request performs a fresh
regulator_get(). There is no "already biased" flag, so whichever get first
finds the rail also runs sunxi_pinctrl_set_io_bias_cfg() (:1062): 3300000 uV
is above 1800000, giving POW_MOD_SEL_2BIT_3V3 = 0b10 at shift 14
(pinctrl-sun60iw2.c:75).

What that costs, measured
-------------------------
GPIO_POW_MOD_SEL 0x02000040 differs between boots of one image on the 8 GiB board,
2026-09-17:

    0x01159570   PH 15:14 = 10 = 3.3 V   consumer under the real vcc-ph rail
    0x01155570   PH 15:14 = 01 = auto    consumer under regulator-dummy

and a boot log of that image
carries three separate "supply vcc-ph not found" lines in a single boot. A
cached regulator cannot log twice, so those are three distinct failed gets;
the fourth one wins and prints nothing, which is why the register moves with
nothing in the log to say so. On a board running the vendor 6.6 kernel the
same register reads 0x01155550, i.e. the vendor stack writes neither field.

So the phandle does not decide whether the rail binds. It decides how the
race fails: with it, a rail not yet registered yields -EPROBE_DEFER
(of_regulator.c:676) which pinctrl-sunxi.c:1046 propagates, so the consumer
-- gmac0, through rgmii0_pins, PH's only claimant in this tree -- retries
deterministically. Without it, the same instant yields the dummy, the -22,
and a silent rebind later.

No new dependency, no new risk
------------------------------
SWOUT2 is regulator-always-on and the eMMC node already consumes it, so the
regulator_enable() at pinctrl-sunxi.c:1050 and the refcounted
regulator_disable() in sunxi_pmx_free() (:1089) cannot drop a rail anyone
else needs -- the same argument `arm64-dts-a733-bank-pc-io-supply` made for CLDO5. Unlike PL, whose
consumer is the I2C bus the PMIC itself sits on, a deferral on PH costs a
retry of the ethernet controller and nothing more.

The patch also corrects the node comment's entry for PB, which `arm64-dts-a733-bank-pc-io-supply` grouped
with PH. PB's case is not "already correct", it is "no field exists": the UM
names bits 25:24 VCCIO_PWR_MOD_SEL and ties them to the VCC-IO net rather
than to a bank, and a733_io_bias_field_shift[] marks PB NO_FIELD
(pinctrl-sun60iw2.c:70). A vcc-pb-supply would replace the -22 warning with
a "left alone" dev_dbg and change no register.

This patch carries no hardware proof yet. What would close it is:
GPIO_POW_MOD_SEL 15:14 reading 0b10 in every boot of a recorded cold-boot
series, no "supply vcc-ph not found" line, and the consumer under the real
rail every time.

It also corrects three entries of the node comment that `arm64-dts-a733-bank-pc-io-supply` wrote, all
refuted since:

 - PB was grouped with PH as "already correct". Its case is "no field
   exists": the UM ties bits 25:24 to the VCC-IO net (VCCIO_PWR_MOD_SEL)
   rather than to a bank, and a733_io_bias_field_shift[] marks PB NO_FIELD
   (pinctrl-sun60iw2.c:70). A vcc-pb-supply would swap the -22 for a
   "left alone" dev_dbg and write no register.
 - PD and PJ were said to hang off BLDO2, with R251 bridging that node to
   SWOUT1. R251 sits between SoC ball 1N7 (PD) and VCC33-LCD; PJ's ball
   1L6 is on VCC33-LCD directly, with no series part; BLDO2 reaches the
   SoC only at ball 1N6 (VCC-LVDS). Same conclusion, different mechanism.
 - PM was said to need a CLDO1 node. Ball 1P22 reaches VCC-PL = ALDO1 at
   3.3 V through RA13 (0R 1%); the net named VCC-PM has two nodes, CLDO1
   and one capacitor, and no SoC ball. A CLDO1 phandle would write
   11 = 1.8 V onto a 3.3 V bank, so that entry was not merely stale.

Comment-only edits, measured rather than assumed: building the DTB, adding
a comment and rebuilding gives a byte-identical blob, so these corrections
cannot change what was put on the board.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun60i-a733-cubie-a7s-minimal.dts
@@ -727,8 +727,27 @@
 			 * (V0.92 read it as Invalid). PE and PK read 00 as
 			 * well, every 3.3 V bank reads 10.
 			 *
-			 * Only PC is wired, and the other six banks that log
-			 * the dummy-regulator line are deliberately left alone:
+			 * Bank PH's pads are supplied from VCC-PH = SWOUT2,
+			 * 3.3 V (per the pinout sheet; the eMMC node already
+			 * consumes the same rail as its vmmc). It is named here
+			 * for a different reason than PC. Without the phandle
+			 * the bank still reaches that rail: the regulator core's
+			 * last-resort lookup matches the requested supply id
+			 * against a registered regulator's regulator-name
+			 * (core.c:2037 -> :1961 -> :103), and SWOUT2's name is
+			 * "vcc-ph". That match is silent and depends on probe
+			 * order, so GPIO_POW_MOD_SEL 15:14 came up 01 (auto) in
+			 * some boots and 10 (3.3 V) in others from one image:
+			 * 0x01155570 and 0x01159570, both measured on the 8 GiB board on
+			 * 2026-09-17. The phandle does not decide whether the
+			 * rail binds, it decides how the race fails -- a rail
+			 * not yet registered then yields -EPROBE_DEFER and the
+			 * consumer (gmac0, via rgmii0_pins) retries, instead of
+			 * taking the dummy and rebinding later without a word.
+			 * 
+			 *
+			 * PC and PH are wired; the banks that still log the
+			 * dummy-regulator line are deliberately left alone:
 			 *
 			 *  - PL is fatal. Its consumer is r_i2c0 (PL0/PL1), the
 			 *    bus the PMIC itself sits on, and it requests its
@@ -740,21 +759,35 @@
 			 *  - PF has no single rail: its supply is selectable
 			 *    (VCC18-PF / VCC-IO) and the SoC-side switch is
 			 *    already owned by GPIO_POW_VAL_SET_CTL, patch `mmc-sunxi-pad-io-voltage-switch-without-vqmmc`.
-			 *  - PJ and PD nominally hang off BLDO2, but R251 (0 R)
-			 *    binds the node to SWOUT1. BLDO2's own register
-			 *    reads 1800 mV on the 8 GiB board and 3300 mV on the 6 GiB board while
-			 *    both banks measure 3.3 V, so the LDO is the wrong
-			 *    phandle and the bridge is not describable in DT.
-			 *  - PM needs a CLDO1 node, and CLDO1 also carries
-			 *    VDD18-LPDDR. A regulator node without
-			 *    regulator-always-on is switched off by
-			 *    regulator_late_cleanup(), so getting that node
-			 *    wrong drops the LPDDR5 1.8 V rail.
-			 *  - PB and PH are already at 3.3 V and read back
-			 *    correctly; wiring them changes nothing observable,
-			 *    so they wait for a reason.
+			 *  - PD and PJ are on VCC33-LCD from SWOUT1, not on
+			 *    BLDO2: ball 1N7 (PD) reaches it through R251
+			 *    (0R 1%) and ball 1L6 (PJ) directly, while BLDO2
+			 *    reaches the SoC only at ball 1N6 (VCC-LVDS). That
+			 *    is why BLDO2's register reads 1800 mV on the 8 GiB board and
+			 *    3300 mV on the 6 GiB board while both banks measure 3.3 V.
+			 *    A SWOUT1 phandle would be describable, but neither
+			 *    bank needs one: both read back correctly and the
+			 *    rail is a load switch with no voltage of its own.
+			 *    (Until 2026-09-18 this comment said R251 bridges a
+			 *    BLDO2 node to SWOUT1. It does not.)
+			 *  - PM is not on CLDO1 at all. Ball 1P22 reaches
+			 *    VCC-PL = ALDO1, 3.3 V, through RA13 (0R 1%); the
+			 *    net named VCC-PM has two nodes, CLDO1 and one
+			 *    capacitor, and no SoC ball. Its withstand field
+			 *    already holds 10 = 3.3 V from the reset default,
+			 *    so an ALDO1 phandle would write what is there and
+			 *    take an enable on the rail that pulls up the PMIC's
+			 *    own I2C bus. A CLDO1 phandle would write 11 = 1.8 V
+			 *    onto a 3.3 V bank. Neither is wired.
+			 *  - PB has no POW_MOD_SEL field at all. Its pads hang
+			 *    on VCC-IO, and the UM names that register's 25:24
+			 *    field VCCIO_PWR_MOD_SEL without tying it to a bank;
+			 *    a733_io_bias_field_shift[] marks PB NO_FIELD. A
+			 *    vcc-pb-supply would only replace the -22 with a
+			 *    "left alone" dev_dbg, so it buys a log line.
 			 */
 			vcc-pc-supply = <&reg_cldo5>;
+			vcc-ph-supply = <&reg_swout2>;
 
 			uart0_pb_pins: uart0-pb-pins {
 				pins = "PB9", "PB10";
